 "drivingQuestion": "Why is the obvious case of the roof stated?",
 "outcome": "resolved",
 "outcomeNote": "Rav Pappa's reading answers the redundancy objection.",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "taanit.22a.16",
   "daf": "22a",
   "sentenceId": 15,
   "he": "גּוּפָא: עָלְתָה לַגַּג וְנָטְלָה תִּינוֹק מֵעֲרִיסָה — מְשׁוּלַּחַת. פְּשִׁיטָא! אָמַר רַב פָּפָּא: כְּכוּכֵי דְצַיָּידֵי.",
   "en": "The Gemara returns to «the» matter of the baraita «itself.» If a wild animal «climbed to the roof and took a baby from» its «cradle,» this is «an outbreak.» The Gemara asks: It «is obvious» that this animal is acting unnaturally. Why does the baraita mention this case? «Rav Pappa said:» The baraita is referring to the roof of «a hunter’s hideout.» Since this hut is in a wild area, one might have thought that it is natural for the beast to attack. Therefore, the baraita teaches us that this is still considered an outbreak."
  }
 ],
